{
  "id": "palindrome-number",
  "questionId": "palindrome-number",
  "slug": "palindrome-number",
  "title": "Palindrome Number",
  "difficulty": "Easy",
  "category": "Two pointers",
  "patterns": [
    "two pointers",
    "math"
  ],
  "lists": [
    "interview-150"
  ],
  "hints": [
    "negative numbers can be rejected immediately - can they ever be palindromes?",
    "you don't need to convert to a string at all; you can peel off digits with x % 10 and x / 10, the same way you'd reverse a whole number",
    "you don't need to reverse the entire number either - stop once the reversed portion is >= what's left of the original, that's the halfway point",
    "watch for numbers ending in 0 (other than 0 itself) - can a palindrome ever have a trailing zero digit that isn't also a leading zero?",
    "at the halfway point, compare the remaining original value to the reversed-so-far value; for odd digit counts, divide the reversed half by 10 first to drop its extra middle digit"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "forgetting that all negative numbers are automatically not palindromes",
    "not handling numbers ending in a non-zero-preceded 0, like 10, 100, 1000 (these can never be palindromes since a leading zero isn't allowed in the original)",
    "reversing the entire number and risking overflow in fixed-width integer languages, when reversing only half avoids that",
    "off-by-one when splitting an odd-length number in half, forgetting to drop the extra middle digit from the reversed portion before comparing"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given an integer `x`.\n\n**Return true if `x` reads the same forward and backward as a base-10 number, and false otherwise.**\n\nAny negative number is never a palindrome, since its written form always starts with a minus sign that has no matching character at the end.\n",
    "constraints": [
      "-2^31 <= x <= 2^31 - 1"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "x is negative (always false)",
      "x is 0 (a single digit, trivially a palindrome)",
      "x is a single digit, non-zero (trivially a palindrome)",
      "x ends in 0 but isn't 0 itself, e.g. 10 or 100 (never a palindrome, since a palindrome can't have a leading zero)"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "x = 121",
        "output": "true",
        "expectedOutput": "true",
        "explanation": "121 reversed is still 121"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "x = -121",
        "output": "false",
        "expectedOutput": "false",
        "explanation": "reversed it would read 121-, which isn't how numbers are written; the leading minus breaks the symmetry"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_3",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "x = 10",
        "output": "false",
        "expectedOutput": "false",
        "explanation": "reversed, 10 becomes 01, which is just 1 - not equal to 10"
      }
    ],
